Other Prediscovery Dispositions
We now turn to means by which a case may end in judgment pre-trial without the consideration of evidence, other than dismissal for failure to state a claim. These other dispositions, like dismissal for failure to state a claim, are not based on consideration of evidence. This book will introduce resolutions based on the evidence later, in Chapter 5 (which is about jury trial) and Chapter 6 (which is about adjudications by the court), but only after we study discovery (in Chapter 4). After all, discovery is how the parties collect their evidence from each other and from other sources.

How is it that a court can resolve a civil action without any consideration of the plaintiff’s allegations or evidence? Only under unusual circumstances, such as when one of the parties doesn’t even show up to court!

INTRODUCTORY NOTE ON DEFAULT JUDGMENT AND RULES 54(c), 55, AND 60
While this chapter has focused on cases in which the defendant responds to a complaint through either answer or a motion to dismiss, there is another alternative available to defendants: do nothing. Rule 55 provides for the entry of default judgment against a defendant who fails to answer or otherwise defend the suit. A default judgment, like any court judgment, involves the entry of a court order resolving the lawsuit’s claims against the defaulting party and, as appropriate, awarding relief. Rule 54(c) elaborates on the relief that the court may grant in a default judgment. A plaintiff, too, may suffer a default judgment if she fails to answer or defend against a counterclaim by a defendant.

Rule 60 provides grounds for relief from judgment. Rule 60 often becomes relevant in the context of default judgments (see Rule 55) but is also relevant as a form of post-trial relief from judgment as well. Keep this in mind when we return to trials and post-trial motions.

